  "poi_santa_cristina_name": "Church of Santa Cristina",
  "poi_santa_cristina_desc": "The church of Santa Cristina was commissioned by Pope Pius VII Chiaramonti and designed by architect Giuseppe Valadier. Construction began in 1816 and ended in 1825; the building is Neoclassical. During the Second World War, the crypt was reinforced and made usable as an air-raid shelter, with capacity for up to 200 people.",
  "poi_rocca_name": "Malatestiana Fortress",
  "poi_rocca_desc": "Construction of the Rocca Nuova, also known as the Malatestiana Fortress, began in 1380 on the initiative of Galeotto Malatesta and continued under Malatesta Novello; it was completed in the late 1470s. In 1502 Cesare Borgia brought Leonardo da Vinci to Cesena to survey the defences of the conquered cities. After losing its military role, the fortress became a prison and remained one until 1969. The public air-raid shelters built in 1944 were at the foot of the hill along Viale Mazzoni, not inside the fortress.",
  "poi_san_rocco_name": "Church of San Rocco",
  "poi_san_rocco_desc": "The parish of San Rocco in Cesena dates back to 1615. In the summer of 1944, the church made its indoor rooms available to people fleeing the bombings, and a shelter was set up in the bell tower. The church was destroyed by the 1944 bombings together with the neighbourhood; only its nineteenth-century bell tower remains today.",
  "poi_abbazia_monte_name": "Abbey of Santa Maria del Monte",
  "poi_abbazia_monte_desc": "The Benedictine abbey of Santa Maria del Monte was founded at the beginning of the 11th century on the hill known as “il Monte”, south-east of Cesena. During the Second World War it sheltered about seven hundred displaced people in its cellars and tunnel. Between 16 and 19 October 1944 it was hit repeatedly and directly by bombing: the church was gutted and part of the monastery collapsed. None of the displaced people sheltering in the abbey were killed.",
  "poi_osservanza_name": "Church and Convent of the Osservanza",
  "poi_osservanza_desc": "The church of the Osservanza was built between 1459 and 1464 at the behest of Malatesta Novello and is linked to the Order of Friars Minor of the Observance. During the Second World War, two air-raid shelters were set up there, in the catacombs and near the bell tower, with a combined capacity of about 200 people. The church was hit by Allied bombing on 16 and 19 October 1944; Father Bosoni died during the raids.",
  "poi_palazzo_ridotto_name": "Palazzo del Ridotto",
  "poi_palazzo_ridotto_desc": "Palazzo del Ridotto stands in Piazza Almerici and was built between 1466 and 1472 as the seat of the municipal authority known as the Conservatori. During the Second World War, an alarm siren was installed on its tower as part of a city-wide warning system, backed up by church bells in the event of a power cut. After the 18 January 1944 attack on the Fascist headquarters in Corso Umberto I, the offices of the Republican Fascist Party moved to the palace, which was also used to detain and torture prisoners.",
  "poi_stazione_name": "Cesena Railway Station",
  "poi_stazione_desc": "Cesena’s first railway station opened in 1861. In 1919 a plan for a larger station building was approved, and the foundation stone of the new station was laid in 1925. The Arrigoni food factory stood next to it, while the Montecatini refinery stood on the opposite side. Because these facilities were close together, the area became a target of Allied bombing after the German occupation. On 29 June 1944 an air raid hit the railway yard, destroyed ammunition loads and set the refinery on fire; further raids followed between July and September.",
  "poi_arrigoni_name": "Arrigoni Factory",
  "poi_arrigoni_desc": "The Arrigoni factory in Cesena stood next to the railway station and became the group’s leading plant for jams, peeled tomatoes and dried minestrone. Over time it employed thousands of people from Cesena and became a centre of local anti-Fascism. Worker Werther Ricchi was employed there before 25 July 1943 and, after 8 September, sent jam and other Arrigoni products to the partisans of the 8th Garibaldi Brigade. He was arrested on 23 March 1944 and tortured.",
  "poi_fantaguzzi_name": "Palazzo Fantaguzzi",
  "poi_fantaguzzi_desc": "Palazzo Fantaguzzi stands in Corso Gastone Sozzi, formerly Corso Umberto I, and has housed the Banca Popolare di Cesena since 1922. During the first months of the Italian Social Republic, part of the building also housed the local Fascist offices. On 18 January 1944 a GAP commando detonated a bomb at the entrance to the Fascist headquarters: there were no casualties, but the building was badly damaged. A few weeks later the Fascist headquarters moved to Palazzo del Ridotto.",
  "poi_rifugi_antiaerei_name": "Air-Raid Shelters of the Fortress",
  "poi_rifugi_antiaerei_desc": "In January 1944, the decision was made to excavate a tunnel in the Rocca hill to create a public air-raid shelter. The tunnel was newly built in 1944 by the city’s chief engineer, Mario Tellerini, and could accommodate 800 people, 290 of them seated. It had two entrances on Viale Mazzoni protected by a blast wall; its ventilation shaft could also serve as an emergency exit.",
